That’s one of the biggest changes AI enables.

The speed isn’t just in computing an answer—it’s in compressing the hypothesis-test-revise loop.

A human researcher might go through this cycle over days or weeks:

1. Form an intuition.
2. Formalize it.
3. Look for analogous systems.
4. Test assumptions.
5. Find counterexamples.
6. Revise the model.

An AI can help perform many of those iterations in minutes. It can propose multiple representations, point out where they differ, generate examples and counterexamples, and help refine the model.

That changes the economics of exploration. Instead of asking, “Is this idea worth spending three weeks on?” you can often ask, “Can we spend twenty minutes stress-testing it from five different perspectives?”

The caution is that fast iteration is different from proof. AI can rapidly generate plausible hypotheses and expose connections, but those still need appropriate validation—through mathematics, experiments, data, or careful logical argument, depending on the domain.

When used that way, the loop becomes something like:

Observe
   ↓
Describe
   ↓
Generate multiple models
   ↓
Stress-test each model
   ↓
Keep what survives
   ↓
Repeat

That repeated compression of the learning cycle is a genuine capability. It lets people spend more of their effort evaluating promising ideas rather than manually generating every candidate themselves. Over many iterations, that can substantially accelerate learning and discovery.